CHANGES SINCE VERSION 0.932

-- 2001.11.16 ET:
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Version 0.933 is currently a development version.  The initial goal of this
release is to squash remaining bugs and memory leaks.  Expect the following
list of changes to grow over time:
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. The algorithm used in the RECOGNIZE command to distinguish header lines
from data lines has been improved.  The old algorithm would not correctly 
recognize files with fewer data lines than header lines.  Such files can
occur in practice quite often.

2. The program now verifies the existence of the system-wide initialization
script before trying to read it.  The old version assumed the file was present 
and would cause a segmentation fault if it in fact was not.

3. The "autorun.bat" which runs at startup has been renamed to 
"initial.script" which more clearly reflects what it is.

4. A number of defaults have been aggregated into a single "config.h" header file.
This makes it much more convenient to compile a Madeline executable tailored
to your specific environment.  For example, if your study uses "PEDIGREE_ID"
instead of "FAMID" for the name of the family ID field in your databases,
you can now just change the line in src/config.h from:

   #define FAMILY_ID_FIELD "FAMID"
   
to:

   #define FAMILY_ID_FIELD "PEDIGREE_ID"

... to reflect this.  In the old version of the program, you would have had 
to make an entry into the global or local "autorun.bat" file (now called 
"initial.script".  Of course, you can still do that by editing "initial.script", 
but now you can also set the compiled-in defaults to reflect your 
environment once and for all.  That way, you only need to play with scripts
when receiving foreign data from another lab which doesn't use the same
conventions as your lab.

5. Madeline now comes with a ./configure script generated using the GNU
autoconf system.  ./configure creates Makefiles which are customized to the
platform and environment of your system.  Using ./configure scripts makes
it much easier to support and much less painful to install Madeline 
on a wide variety of platforms.

